.\" raz(1) - the raz compiler
.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt RAZ 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm raz
.Nd compile a raz program to a Linux executable
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar option ...
.Ar Main.raz
.Nm
.Cm run
.Op Ar option ...
.Ar Main.raz
.Op Ar arg ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
compiles the module in
.Ar Main.raz ,
and every module it imports, to an executable for x86-64 Linux, with
no assembler, linker or C library in between.
A program that names a C library in a
.Ic link
line, or declares an
.Ic extern
procedure, is linked against C dynamically; any other is static.
.Pp
A module
.Ar Name
is found as
.Ar Name Ns .linux-x86_64.raz
or
.Ar Name Ns .raz
in the directory of
.Ar Main.raz ,
then in each
.Fl I
directory in turn, then in the library,
.Pa lib/
beside the
.Nm
executable, whose modules
.Xr raz-lib 3
says.
A program has one module of each name: a module found in two of these
places is an error, unless one of the files is a symbolic link to the
other, which chooses it.
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm run
compiles the same way, then runs the program with the
.Ar arg Ns s ,
writing no file: its output and exit status are those of the command.
.Pp
Every array index and slice is checked, unless the module says
.Ic check off ;
a failed check, a nil pointer, a division by zero or a stack overflow
ends the program with status 2, and says on standard error where it
was, the calls under way, and their variables:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Bad.raz:6:7: index 5 is out of range for a length of 3
  in Bad.main at Bad.raz:6:7: i = 5
.Ed
.Pp
The executable has a symbol table and line numbers for
.Xr gdb 1 ,
.Xr nm 1
and
.Xr perf 1 ,
unless built with
.Fl s .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-noprofile"
.It Fl o Ar file
Write the executable to
.Ar file .
Left out, it is
.Ar Main.raz
without
.Pa .raz .
.Cm run
takes no
.Fl o .
.It Fl I Ar dir
Look for imported modules in
.Ar dir
too, after the main module's own directory and before the library.
It may be given up to 16 times.
.It Fl Werror
A warning counts as an error.
.It Fl S
List the main module's machine code, each instruction under the line
of source it was made for, on standard output, and write no
executable.
.It Fl s
Strip the program of what is there for finding faults: its symbol
table, its line numbers, the places a failure is told by, and the
profiler.
A failure then says only its address, and the program is much
smaller: hello, world is 7 KB, against 28 KB.
.It Fl noprofile
Leave out the profiler, which
.Ev RAZ_PROFILE
turns on, but keep the rest.
.It Fl h , Fl \-help
Say the options.
.It Fl \-version
Say the version of
.Nm ,
and the version of the report it follows.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "RAZ_PROFILE"
.It Ev RAZ_PROFILE
Set to anything, in the environment of a program built without
.Fl s
or
.Fl noprofile ,
it has the program sample itself every millisecond of its time, and
say at its end, on standard error, which procedures took the most of
it, each with its share in its own code and its share in all, with the
procedures it called, and which places in the source:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
profile: 521 samples of a millisecond; each procedure's own, then with its calls'
   16%     27%  Screen.put
    9%     93%  Parser.feed
by place:
    8%  src/Screen.raz:275:5
.Ed
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 if it wrote the program, and 1 if it found an error, which it
says on standard error, with the line of source and a mark under the
place.
.Nm
.Cm run
exits as the program does.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compile and run hello, world:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
raz Hello.raz && ./Hello
raz run Hello.raz
.Ed
.Pp
Build a small program for shipping, then see where its time goes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
raz -s -o hello Hello.raz
raz -o prog Prog.raz && RAZ_PROFILE=1 ./prog
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr raz-lib 3 ,
the library;
.Xr raz-tutorial 7 ,
a tour of the language;
.Xr raz 7 ,
the language at a glance;
.Xr gdb 1 .
The report,
.Pa doc/raz.md ,
defines the language, and the guide to the compiler,
.Pa doc/compiler.md ,
walks through its sources.
